WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
SPEAKER: A huge part
of maintaining security

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.740
in the cloud is enforcing
the age-old principle

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.120
of least privilege.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.189
Users and service
accounts should only

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.650
have the minimum permissions
needed to perform

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.360
what they actually need to do.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.840
If you're involved with Identity
and Access Management, or IAM,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.880
how can you be sure that excess
permissions aren't granted?

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.090
And as your workload
evolves over time,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.280
how do if the initial set
of permissions assigned

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.530
are appropriate or if you
need to make some changes?

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.250
That's where Active Assist
Policy Intelligence and IAM

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.070
Recommender can help.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.560
Users and applications
access the resources

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.810
in your Google Cloud
projects by authenticating

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.730
as users or service accounts.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.220
We refer to these
accounts as principals.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.110
The level of access
of each principal

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.530
is determined by the permission
specified in the IAM policy.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.380
These permissions are grouped
into distinct IAM roles

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.750
to make it easy to assign
access based on different needs.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.118
For example, the Compute
Network Admin role

00:00:54.118 --> 00:00:56.660
is a built-in role that grants
full control of Compute Engine

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.190
networking resources.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.510
This role includes more
than 400 permissions,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.650
such as all the permissions to
create and manage subnetworks.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.200
The Compute Network User
role, another built-in role

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.540
for using, but not managing,
Compute Engine networking

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.210
resources, has less than
a hundred permissions.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.550
Any principle with this
role can list and use

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:17.610
existing subnetworks, but not
create, modify, or delete them.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.963
Meanwhile, the Compute
Network Viewer role

00:01:19.963 --> 00:01:21.380
grants over a
hundred permissions,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.750
including the permission
for listing health checks.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.210
Each of these roles provides
different levels of access

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.250
to the same set of resources.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.800
As you can imagine, managing
many principals and roles

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.410
for a collection of resources
can become pretty complex

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:36.710
pretty quickly and might result
in too much or too little

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:37.700
access.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.190
Does the first user actually
need the Compute Network Admin

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:40.730
role?

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.620
And is the Compute
Network User role

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.750
enough access for
the second user?

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.490
Could a malicious
actor take advantage

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.280
of over-granted permissions
to cause damage?

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.920
Thankfully, the recommendations
from Active Assist Policy

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.130
Intelligence can help us
answer questions like these.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.140
Policy Intelligence
is a suite of tools

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.990
under the Active Assist
Intelligent Tools category.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.487
These policy
intelligence tools are

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.320
designed to help you
make informed decisions

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.450
about the IAM policies
on your project

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.720
and perform audits and
compliance-related tasks.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.759
Policy Analyzer
helps you understand

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:13.550
who has access to which
Google Cloud resources.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:17.030
Policy Simulator shows you
how a change to an IAM policy

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.680
might impact the
principal's existing

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.710
access before the
change is actually made.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.470
Policy Troubleshooter
examines all IAM policies

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.210
that apply to a
resource to show you

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.850
why a principal might not
have access and the best way

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:29.960
to fix it.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.960
And finally, there's the IAM
Recommender, which offers you

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.650
two primary benefits.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.350
First, it identifies
the principals

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.020
that might have excess
permissions granted to them.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.480
The Recommender spots those
excess permissions and uses

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.610
machine learning to
predict future usage.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.310
This information is saved
as Policy Insights for you

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.250
to understand your policy
usage at the project, folder,

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.230
or organization level.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.060
The excess permissions
are identified

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.460
by comparing the permissions
that each principal has used

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.040
in the past 90 days with
the total permissions

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.810
that that principal has.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.570
Second, the IAM Recommender
assesses each role

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.340
granted to a
principal to determine

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.560
if there's a better fit, while
optimizing for least privilege.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.980
These suggestions could be
for a more appropriate role,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.490
multiple roles, or remove
all the permissions together.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.620
Suggested roles might
include built-in roles,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.050
like the ones that we've
mentioned earlier, or custom

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:18.920
roles.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.475
Custom roles can include any
combination of permissions

00:03:21.475 --> 00:03:22.850
so there's a lot
of ways that you

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.560
can control the
roles and permissions

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.155
that your principals have.

00:03:26.155 --> 00:03:28.280
Let's take a look at what
the IAM Recommender looks

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.900
like from the Cloud Console.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.650
For this demonstration, I have
the IAM Recommender Admin role

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.250
to view and manage the IAM
recommendations and the Project

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.760
IAM Admin role so that I can
update the project IAM policy

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.720
with those recommendations.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.540
Let's take a look
at the IAM and Admin

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.930
page, where the IAM policies
for our project, folder,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.380
and organizations are managed.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.540
This view is for our
project, where we've got

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.520
some recommendations available.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.190
At the top, we'll find a
banner with some priority

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:00.800
recommendations.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.110
We can click here to add a
filter to our table below.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.870
The light bulb icon in the
Security Insights column

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.870
means that our ML models have
identified a recommendation

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.870
for that principal.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.209
For example, this
particular service account

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:18.140
has the Editor role
assigned to it,

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.990
which is a role with
a lot of permissions,

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.840
as you can see here.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.480
The recommendation here is
to replace the Editor role

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.570
with the Storage Object Admin
role for this principal.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:30.700
On the right are the
individual permissions

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.550
that will be removed if
making the recommended change.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.270
We can accept the recommendation
here by clicking Apply.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.910
That recommendation will now
appear on the Recommendations

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.212
History page.

00:04:44.212 --> 00:04:46.420
Here's where we can see how
many recommendations have

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:50.360
been applied and
what the details are.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.980
When we select the
recommendation,

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.870
there's a summary of
the change, including

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.310
the list of the permissions
that we saw before.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.990
If anything were
to come up and we

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.970
would need to undo
the change, we

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:05.070
can click Revert to
restore the permissions.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.600
For another view of our
IAM recommendations,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.010
we can head over to the
Recommendations Hub.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.350
Since we only have the
IAM Recommendation Admin

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.240
role assigned, those
are the only kinds

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.140
of recommendations that
will show up on this screen.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.930
Clicking View All shows us
all of the recommendations

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.430
matching this category.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.410
And selecting one of
those recommendations

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.600
will give us additional details.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.610
Just as before, we
can review the changes

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.722
the recommendation
suggests, including

00:05:33.722 --> 00:05:35.180
the permission for
the current role

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.430
and the permissions for the
recommended replacement role.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.640
If our IAM policies are managed
with the infrastructure-as-code

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.790
solution Terraform, we
would click Use Terraform

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.670
to see the proposed change as
a code snippet in Terraform

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.190
configuration.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.760
Otherwise, we can just click
Apply to update the project IAM

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.060
policy with the role change.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.940
The IAM Recommender helps you
improve your security posture

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.030
on Google Cloud, focusing on
the principle of least privilege

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.640
with a set of machine-learning
powered analyses.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.290
We saw what these
recommendations look

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:09.820
like from the Google
Cloud Console,

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.890
but you can also
access and manage them

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:15.438
through the API and Google
Cloud Command Line interface.

00:06:15.438 --> 00:06:16.980
You can find links
in the description

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.050
for more information
on policy intelligence

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.230
and using the IAM Recommender
as part of the Active Assist

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.208
portfolio.

00:06:23.208 --> 00:06:24.750
Don't forget about
the Recommendation

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.410
Hub, where you can see all sorts
of helpful recommendations.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.910
Stay tuned for the next video,
where we'll look at some ways

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.535
that you can take
these recommendations

00:06:31.535 --> 00:06:32.820
and apply them at scale.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.670
See you there.

